# Attack-path analysis
Final: ignore | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Although the finding claims a missed-notification race, the code shows waiters lock the generation mutex while still holding the per-PG mutex (bucket_ops.rs:54-71, 414-454). State changes that notify require the same PG mutex and increment generation under the generation lock (bucket_ops.rs:590-610; node.rs:427-432), and get_pg is a MutexGuard (node.rs:583-590). This ordering prevents a notify from occurring before the waiter samples generation, so the race is not reachable; impact and likelihood are effectively none.
## Likelihood
ignore - Race condition is not reachable under the existing PG mutex and generation lock ordering.
## Impact
ignore - The alleged missed-notification hang is prevented by lock ordering; no proven availability impact from this code path.
## Assumptions
- Default listen address (127.0.0.1:9000) is used unless operators override ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR.
- Bucket drain/write-reservation paths are reachable through authenticated S3 operations (e.g., bucket delete or write).
- No out-of-tree code alters the PG mutex and bucket_coordination lock ordering.
- Valid S3 credentials (access key/secret) for write/delete operations
- Trigger bucket drain/write reservation paths
- Concurrent operations to create a draining/reservation wait
## Path
Client -> HTTP frontend -> bucket_ops -> condvar wait (missed notify blocked by PG mutex ordering)
## Path evidence
- `crates/storage/src/node/bucket_ops.rs:54-71` - Wait path for begin_bucket_write_drain locks generation while holding bucket_pg, then drops bucket_pg before waiting.
- `crates/storage/src/node/bucket_ops.rs:414-454` - Write-reservation retry path uses the same lock ordering (generation lock held before dropping bucket_pg).
- `crates/storage/src/node/bucket_ops.rs:590-610` - State changes (release reservation/end drain) acquire bucket_pg and then call notify_bucket_coordination_change after dropping PG lock.
- `crates/storage/src/node.rs:583-590` - get_pg returns a MutexGuard over PgStore, establishing per-PG mutual exclusion for metadata changes.
- `crates/storage/src/node.rs:427-432` - notify_bucket_coordination_change increments generation under the generation mutex and notifies all waiters.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:28-65` - Configuration requires access/secret keys and defaults listen address to 127.0.0.1:9000, indicating authenticated, local-default exposure.
## Narrative
The reported missed-notification race is not reachable because the waiters take the generation mutex while still holding the per-PG mutex, then drop the PG mutex before waiting; any state change that would notify must first acquire the same PG mutex and then increments generation under the generation lock. This ordering prevents a notify from occurring before the waiter samples the generation, eliminating the alleged race and DoS condition.
## Controls
- Per-PG mutex serialization for metadata changes (SharedStorageNode::get_pg)
- Generation mutex + condvar for bucket coordination
- Credential-based auth configured via ARGMIN_ACCESS_KEY_ID/ARGMIN_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
## Blindspots
- Static analysis only; no concurrent runtime stress tests executed here.
- Deployment exposure (public vs local bind) is environment-dependent and not evidenced in IaC/manifests.
